1
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:12,479
This is what a robot arm's camera sees: a table, a few everyday things, and a camera looking down at them.

2
00:00:12,479 --> 00:00:19,270
Robots now decide what to grab by asking a vision-language model what it sees.

3
00:00:19,270 --> 00:00:20,750
So let's ask one.

4
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:30,057
Here is that photo, and the exact question: is there room to place the tape on the table between the bottle and the lime?

5
00:00:30,057 --> 00:00:32,750
Claude answers: {{REAL_TRAP_ANSWER}}.

6
00:00:33,250 --> 00:00:38,558
But there is room. The gap is 6.9 centimetres and the tape is 4.5.

7
00:00:38,558 --> 00:00:44,750
The tape sits nearer the lens, so in the picture it looks wider than the gap.

8
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:50,693
Here is the same table as a 3D digital twin, built from the scene file.

9
00:00:50,693 --> 00:00:53,453
Turn it and the gap is plain to see.

10
00:00:53,453 --> 00:00:56,750
The picture misleads; the 3D truth doesn't.

11
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:00,750
We didn't find one trick question. We built a factory.

12
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:12,750
One recipe and a seed give thousands of tasks: photoreal scenes, three robot cameras, twelve kinds of question.

13
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:20,217
Every answer comes from a program on the scene file, and eight gates check it again from the pixels.

14
00:01:20,217 --> 00:01:21,750
No AI in the checking.

15
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:28,096
Which object is nearest the gripper? In the picture, the box.

16
00:01:28,096 --> 00:01:33,750
In 3D, the jug. Every Claude model we tested picks the box.

17
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:41,139
Across 1,567 tasks, every Claude model we tested is about 25 points worse when the 2D picture misleads.

18
00:01:41,139 --> 00:01:44,750
More thinking doesn't help: the gap moves by 0 points.

19
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:52,500
Move one object and ask again: they get both versions right only 34 to 52% of the time.

20
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,750
They don't track the scene.

21
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:59,080
Hide the evidence and they lose 10 to 13 points.

22
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:02,750
A harsh camera costs Sonnet and Opus 12 to 16.

23
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:07,448
Re-render it and the truth is identical.

24
00:02:07,448 --> 00:02:11,750
One command gives a judge the same tasks.

25
00:02:12,250 --> 00:02:18,750
}

26
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:31,750
}

27
00:02:32,250 --> 00:02:34,507
Robots will act on these answers.

28
00:02:34,507 --> 00:02:38,064
Our factory finds the failure before the robot does.

29
00:02:38,064 --> 00:02:46,750
It's a test, a source of training data, and a verifiable reward for robot learning: what Innodata's physical-AI business needs.
